militia-operation-random-description-01 = The vessel requested passage using the registration of a decommissioned tug. Intelligence places a Rassvet group and its cell's records aboard. The inspection team will verify the cover story.
militia-operation-random-description-02 = A Rassvet dispatch was mistakenly sent to a departmental relay. The intended recipient has already asked us to forget it. Investigators would prefer to examine the originals aboard.
militia-operation-random-description-03 = The object answers every query with the same message, including an identical cough at the fourth second. Communications staff find the recording convincing, but rather overworked. Intelligence confirms the vessel belongs to Rassvet.
militia-operation-random-description-04 = Two independent reports mention the same Rassvet vessel. The route, departure time and contact's surname match. Operational records aboard are needed to advance the investigation.
militia-operation-random-description-05 = An unregistered vessel is avoiding shipping lanes and repeatedly changing its callsign. The latest callsign already belongs to another ship, whose captain is extremely displeased. Intelligence has linked the crew to Rassvet.
militia-operation-random-description-06 = An intercepted argument concerned who forgot to destroy the official papers. Transmissions stopped after someone mentioned the militia. The source has been located: an armed Rassvet vessel.
militia-operation-random-description-07 = Transport inspectors found a discrepancy between the object's declared route and actual course. Checks through restricted channels identified a Rassvet cell. Its shipboard records are of interest to investigators.
militia-operation-random-description-08 = An agent reported a vessel used by Rassvet for meetings and exchanging orders. The source has not made contact since the last transmission. Observation confirms the coordinates; further inspection falls to the militia.
militia-operation-random-description-09 = The vessel claimed to be a repair team but could not name the facility it was servicing. The dispatcher was advised to stop interfering. Its radio signature matches a previously identified Rassvet group.
militia-operation-random-description-10 = Seized correspondence revealed coordinates for Rassvet's next meeting. Observation confirms a vessel in the area. Its records must be seized before the information reaches other cells.
militia-operation-random-description-11 = A suspicious vessel maintained complete radio silence, then requested a frequency for ordering dinner. Its transmitter matches the Rassvet intercept database. Investigators are interested in the crew's papers, rather than its catering.
militia-operation-random-description-12 = Several separate payments led investigators to a single vessel. Recipients changed, but the intermediary did not. An armed Rassvet group operates aboard; its papers may reveal the rest of the chain.
militia-operation-random-description-13 = The object reported faulty identification equipment while maintaining clear communications on a restricted Rassvet frequency. This selective technical failure was deemed suspicious. An armed inspection has been authorized.
militia-operation-random-description-14 = A Rassvet dispatch reports a group moving with a package of internal orders. Observation identified a matching vessel near the facility. Original documents must be delivered to investigators.
militia-operation-random-description-15 = A suspicious vessel requested passage three times under different organizations. All three requests contain the same typo. Checks exposed a Rassvet cover; an inspection team will deliver the reply in person.
militia-operation-random-description-16 = A source reported another planned movement of Rassvet forces. The vessel was detected before leaving the area. Its operational records should reveal the group's next steps.
militia-operation-random-description-17 = An obsolete Rassvet password was transmitted on an open channel. The transmission was cut off, but the coordinates had already been logged. A militia inspection team is being dispatched.
militia-operation-random-description-18 = Detaining a courier allowed investigators to reconstruct part of the route used by their contacts. An armed Rassvet vessel was found at its endpoint. Seizing its records will help verify the courier's testimony.
militia-operation-random-description-19 = The vessel's command claimed the ship was empty. Its next message asked us to stop making queries because the crew was resting. Intelligence confirms Rassvet personnel aboard and advises against expecting hospitality.
militia-operation-random-description-20 = An observation post detected a brief transmission from a vessel previously implicated in supplying Rassvet. The message could not be decoded. Shipboard documents may prove more useful than the intercept.
militia-operation-random-description-21 = A forged servicing request bears the signature of an employee who has worked in another sector for years. The object's Rassvet links were independently confirmed. Investigators request the original records held aboard.
militia-operation-random-description-22 = A Rassvet vessel was detected while comparing old coordinates with current observations. It has remained in place longer than expected. Intelligence recommends taking advantage of the delay to conduct an inspection.
militia-operation-random-description-23 = An intercepted Rassvet order instructed personnel to check internal records and stop discussing them over public channels. The final instruction was broadcast on a public channel. The source was located and the case passed to the militia.
militia-operation-random-description-24 = The vessel refuses document checks, citing instructions from a nonexistent agency. The agency's name changes with every query. Restricted intelligence places Rassvet fighters and command records aboard.
militia-operation-random-description-25 = Information on the object was assembled from reports, transport logs and one particularly talkative intermediary. Its Rassvet affiliation is beyond doubt. Investigators need its operational records, rather than another promise to send them voluntarily.
